# Pinned tool versions for the bare-metal probe box.
#
# CONSUMED BY EXACTLY ONE FILE: infra/provision/common-system.sh.
#
# It is NOT read by infra/lima/overdrive-dev.yaml — Lima keeps its own `param:`
# block, hand-maintained, and the two are kept aligned by eye. Saying otherwise
# here would be a claim the code does not support.
#
# Shell-sourceable: `set -a; . infra/provision/versions.env; set +a`

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE RULE FOR EVERY PIN IN THIS FILE: track LATEST STABLE.
#
# A pin below latest requires a NAMED BLOCKING REASON written here, plus the
# condition that would clear it. "Our tests pass on the pinned version" is NOT
# a reason — that exact sentence is what held this file on Cloud Hypervisor
# v46.0 for 14 months and 7 releases. The worked example is kept below rather
# than deleted.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Cloud Hypervisor.
CLOUD_HYPERVISOR_VERSION="v53.0"

# WAS "v46.0" until 2026-08-10. Retained as a cautionary note, because the
# comment that held it there READ like rigour and was the opposite:
#
#   > do NOT adopt the "CH >= 48.0" floor from the opencapsule reference
#   > implementation. That number appears in six of its documents with no
#   > stated reason [...] and v46.0 demonstrably has the capability we
#   > actually need.
#
# The first half was correct — their floor was unjustified. The second half is
# a non sequitur: the right response to an unjustified floor is to take LATEST,
# not to keep the oldest build that passes today's tests. It froze the pin
# against the requirements known at that moment (it boots; it has `--landlock`)
# and argued down the one prompt that would have moved it.
#
# What that cost, concretely: checkpoint/restore later became the target
# workload, and `vhost-user-fs` restore is BROKEN on v46 —
# cloud-hypervisor#6931, independently reproduced at v47.0.0, VMM hangs at
# `Restoring vhost-user-fs _fs0`. Fixed in v52.0 (#7908 vhost-user restore
# filled out, #7937 virtio-fs migration, #7851 resume interrupt). v46.0 shipped
# 2025-05-23; the bump happened 14 months later.
#
# v53.0 verified on the metal box to retain every flag we depend on:
#   --landlock  --landlock-rules  --seccomp  --api-socket  --vsock  --restore

# virtiofsd comes from apt (Ubuntu ships 1.13.2), NOT a pinned download.
# It lands at /usr/libexec/virtiofsd and is NOT on $PATH — `which virtiofsd`
# returns nothing. Anything invoking it must use the absolute path.
VIRTIOFSD_BIN="/usr/libexec/virtiofsd"

# NOTE: wasmtime and pwru are deliberately absent. Lima installs both; this box
# does not, because nothing in the microVM probes uses them. They were listed
# here briefly as dead variables, which implied the metal box got them. It does
# not — add them to common-system.sh first if that changes.
